From: omaflake contributors
Subject: virtio-gpu-virgl: flush context 0 after 2D transfers

With the cocoa display on desktop GL (-display cocoa,gl=on), virgl's
context 0 and the display's CALayer context are separate CGL contexts in
one share group. TRANSFER_TO_HOST_2D uploads the guest's pixels with
glTexSubImage2D in context 0, and nothing flushes that context afterwards:
3D guests flush through their fences, but 2D-only scanouts (UEFI GOP,
systemd-boot, the kernel's fbdev console) never do. CGL makes a shared
object's new contents visible to another context only after the writing
context has flushed, so the display samples the texture as created
(black) until the first 3D client's submissions flush context 0.

Flush context 0 after each 2D transfer. The EGL path (gl=es, ANGLE) does
not show the problem.

diff --git a/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c b/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
index d37bbd8a4..33434af95 100644
--- a/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
+++ b/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
@@ -698,6 +698,17 @@ static void virgl_cmd_transfer_to_host_2d(VirtIOGPU *g,
                                       0,
                                       (struct virgl_box *)&box,
                                       t2d.offset, NULL, 0);
+
+    /*
+     * The upload runs as glTexSubImage2D in virgl's context 0, but the
+     * display samples the texture from its own (shared) context. Shared
+     * contexts only see the new contents once the writing context has
+     * flushed (Apple's CGL rule for shared contexts). Nothing else flushes
+     * context 0 for 2D-only guests (firmware, simpledrm/fbcon, Plymouth),
+     * so their scanouts stay black until a 3D client submits commands.
+     */
+    virgl_renderer_force_ctx_0();
+    glFlush();
 }
 
 static void virgl_cmd_transfer_to_host_3d(VirtIOGPU *g,
